.TH ROCKETSMBD 8 "2026-09-27" "rocketsmbd" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
rocketsmbd \- io_uring SMB2/SMB3 file server (zero-copy, multichannel)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rocketsmbd
[\fB\-\-config\fR \fIpath\fR]
[\fB\-\-check\fR]
[\fB\-\-version\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rocketsmbd
is a from-scratch SMB2/3 file server built on Linux io_uring. Accept, receive,
send, and file I/O all flow through one io_uring per worker thread; file reads
are served zero-copy from page cache to socket via linked
.BR splice (2)
operations. It speaks SMB 2.0.2 through 3.1.1 with NTLMv2 and (in a
\fBkerberos\fR build) Kerberos authentication, SMB2/3 signing, SMB3
multichannel, SMB3 encryption (AES-128/256-GCM and -CCM), byte-range locks,
change notification, and read/handle-caching leases. It serves SMB on TCP 445
only (direct TCP; no NetBIOS port 139). An HTTP health endpoint is available
only when \fBhealth_listen\fR is set.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-config " " \fIpath\fR " (or " \-c ")"
Path to the TOML configuration file. Default: \fIrocketsmbd.toml\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-check
Validate the configuration and exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-version " (or " \-V ")"
Print version and exit.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration is TOML, by convention \fI/etc/rocketsmbd.toml\fR.
.TP
.B listen
Bind address, default \fB"0.0.0.0:445"\fR. Binding 445 needs CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE.
.TP
.B workers
Worker threads (one io_uring + SO_REUSEPORT listener each). 0 = one per core.
.TP
.B server_name
Advertised server name.
.TP
.B log_level
0 = warn, 1 = info, 2 = debug.
.TP
.B allow_guest
Allow unauthenticated guest sessions. Unset: true only when no users exist and
Kerberos isn't configured (no enabled \fB[kerberos]\fR table, \fBauth\fR not
\fB"kerberos"\fR).
.TP
.B require_signing
Reject unsigned requests on authenticated sessions. Unset means false and logs
a warning; the default becomes true in 2.0. Recommended: true.
.TP
.B multichannel
Advertise SMB3 multichannel and accept session binding (SMB 3.x, to an
established non-guest session only).
.TP
.B advertise_only
List of IPs to advertise for multichannel (e.g. a storage NIC). Empty = all
non-loopback interfaces.
.TP
.B encrypt
Require SMB3 encryption on all post-auth traffic. A session that can't be
encrypted (SMB 2.x/3.0.x, SMB 3.1.1 without a cipher, or guest) is refused,
and a plaintext request on an encrypted session gets ACCESS_DENIED. Default
false; encryption a client asks for (e.g. cifs \fBseal\fR) is honored either
way.
.TP
.B prefer_aes256
Prefer AES-256 ciphers (GCM, then CCM) when offered. Default false.
.TP
.B oplocks
Grant read- and handle-caching leases. Default true. Write-caching is never
granted.
.TP
.B core_pinning
Pin worker N to CPU core N mod ncpu. Default true.
.TP
.B sqpoll
Enable io_uring SQPOLL (one busy kernel thread per worker). Default false.
.TP
.B health_listen
Address for an HTTP health endpoint, for example \fB"127.0.0.1:9104"\fR.
Unset by default (no listener). \fBGET /healthz\fR returns 200 while every
worker is running and every share path is a directory, 503 otherwise, with a
JSON body. Bind it to loopback or an admin network.
.TP
.B auth
\fB"both"\fR (default; Kerberos preferred, NTLM fallback), \fB"ntlm"\fR, or
\fB"kerberos"\fR. Intersected with the mechanisms the binary was built with.
.PP
All top-level keys must appear before the first table header. Unknown keys are
rejected.
.PP
Shares are \fB[[share]]\fR tables with \fBname\fR, \fBpath\fR (an existing
directory), and optional \fBread_only\fR; at least one is required and
\fBIPC$\fR is reserved. Users are \fB[[user]]\fR tables with \fBname\fR and
either \fBpassword\fR or a 32-hex-char \fBnt_hash\fR. An optional
\fB[kerberos]\fR table sets \fBenabled\fR, \fBkeytab\fR, \fBspn\fR (default
\fIcifs/<server_name>\fR), and \fBrealm\fR (qualifies bare user names in
share lists).
.PP
A share may restrict access with \fBvalid_users\fR, \fBinvalid_users\fR and
\fBread_only_users\fR, checked at tree connect: \fBinvalid_users\fR denies,
a non-empty \fBvalid_users\fR denies everyone it doesn't match, and
\fBread_only_users\fR makes the share read-only. Entries are a local user
(\fIalice\fR, which also matches \fIalice@<realm>\fR), a Kerberos principal
(\fIalice@REALM\fR), a PAC user (\fIDOMAIN\ealice\fR), or a group:
\fI@S-1-5-21-...\fR, a well-known AD group such as \fI@Domain Admins\fR, or
\fI@name\fR for a \fB[[group]]\fR table (\fBname\fR, \fBsid\fR and/or
\fBmembers\fR). AD group membership comes from the verified Kerberos PAC;
there is no LDAP lookup.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
listen = "0.0.0.0:445"
workers = 0
multichannel = true

[[share]]
name = "data"
path = "/srv/data"

[[user]]
name = "alice"
password = "secret"
.fi
.PP
Mount from a client:
.PP
.nf
mount -t cifs //server/data /mnt -o username=alice,password=secret,vers=3.1.1
.fi
.SH SECURITY
A self-review was done in October 2026 (docs/SECURITY-REVIEW.md), but no
external security review yet; do not expose port 445 to the public internet. Authorization is per share (the access lists above); file I/O
runs as the server's Unix user, so on-disk permissions don't distinguish
clients. The packaged systemd unit runs it as the unprivileged \fBrocketsmbd\fR
user (only \fBCAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE\fR): share trees, and a keytab, must be
accessible to that user. For untrusted
networks, use Kerberos or NTLMv2 users with \fBrequire_signing\fR and
\fBencrypt\fR. See SECURITY.md.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/lib/sysusers.d/rocketsmbd.conf
Defines the \fBrocketsmbd\fR service user the packages create.
.TP
.I /etc/rocketsmbd.toml
Configuration file installed by the packages and used by the systemd unit.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mount.cifs (8)
